 \begin{abstract}
-We give an elementary finite-jet treatment of sign laws prescribed on whole orthants and on chambers of finite real affine hyperplane arrangements. A first nonzero homogeneous Taylor term inherits the prescribed signs weakly; polynomial factor multiplicities then force a signed parity law. A local-overlap argument propagates the crossing parity along each hyperplane. The resulting $C^\infty$ obstruction is an instance of the established signature-flatness theory of Broglia and Tognoli, which also contains a leading-Taylor-term parity argument in a special crossing example. The contribution here is a systematic elementary arrangement-level proof, explicit finite-regularity forms (including a $C^N$ arrangement version), and a synthesis with ultradifferentiable realization.
+Let $\mathcal A$ be a finite real affine hyperplane arrangement. We study which $\{\pm1\}$-valued chamber labelings can occur as the sign of a function with prescribed regularity. A first nonzero homogeneous Taylor term inherits the chamber signs weakly, and factor multiplicities force a constant crossing parity. This gives a short arrangement-level proof of the known smooth signature-flatness obstruction and, in finite regularity, explicit $C^d$ orthant and $C^N$ arrangement rigidity statements.
 
-In compactwise Roumieu Denjoy--Carleman classes, the classical quasianalyticity criterion and positive flat functions yield a dichotomy: precisely affine Boolean laws on whole orthants in the quasianalytic case, and all Boolean laws in the non-quasianalytic case. For $m$ distinct hyperplanes with $r$ chambers, the corresponding counts are $2^{m+1}$ and $2^r$. We place these statements in the older literature on distributions of signs and signatures, and in the Varchenko--Gelfand tradition of chamber functions. The emphasis is proof economy, normalization, and a transparent bridge among finite jets, sign rigidity, and ultradifferentiable realizability rather than historical firstness of the smooth obstruction.
+For normalized compactwise Roumieu Denjoy--Carleman classes $\mathcal E^{\{M\}}$, the Denjoy--Carleman criterion yields an exact realization dichotomy. In the quasianalytic case the realizable chamber laws are precisely the signed parity laws; in the non-quasianalytic case every chamber labeling is realized by explicit selector formulas. Thus an arrangement of $m$ distinct hyperplanes with $r$ chambers admits exactly $2^{m+1}$ and $2^r$ realizable sign laws in the two regimes. The smooth obstruction and positive-flat existence mechanisms are classical; the contribution is the finite-order formulation, self-contained affine-arrangement proof, and unified exact realization classification.
 \end{abstract}
 
