-\subsection{Sign distributions, polynomial signs, and chamber functions}
-
-The factor-parity mechanism in Lemma~\ref{thm:T1} is classical antecedent material, not the paper's novelty. In Broglia--Tognoli's Proposition~2.4, maximal powers of irreducible defining polynomials determine whether a polynomial-induced signature changes sign along each component; for hyperplanes this is exactly the parity-bit mechanism used here~\cite[Proposition~2.4]{BrogliaTognoli1989}. A later 2014 MathOverflow discussion gives the same observation in coordinate-orthant language: a polynomial required to switch sign across one facet but not another facet of the same coordinate hyperplane would force the multiplicity of the corresponding coordinate factor to be both odd and even~\cite{MathOverflow2014}. There is also a separate literature on polynomial sign representations of Boolean functions on finite grids; for example, Basu, Bhatnagar, Gopalan, and Lipton study degree--sparsity tradeoffs for sign representations of parity~\cite{BasuEtAl2008}. Those finite-grid questions are different from requiring a sign throughout every point of each open orthant.
-
-The more direct historical language is that of sign distributions and signatures. Fortuna~\cite{Fortuna1991} considers sign assignments to components of complements of codimension-one algebraic sets. Broglia and Tognoli~\cite{BrogliaTognoli1989} connect nonadmissible signatures with flatness; Acquistapace and Broglia continue this line in \emph{Signatures and flatness}, together with its published correction~\cite{AcquistapaceBroglia1992,AcquistapaceBroglia1993Correction}. The local-completion problem on algebraic surfaces is studied by Acquistapace, Broglia, and Fortuna~\cite{AcquistapaceBrogliaFortuna1994}, while Acquistapace and Broglia relate signatures to approximation by regular or analytic functions~\cite{AcquistapaceBroglia1994}. The present arrangement statements should therefore be read as an elementary specialization and finite-jet repackaging of this tradition, not as the origin of the sign-rigidity phenomenon.
