-
-The background literature contains several stronger analytic frameworks. Coordinate division is part of standard axiomatic treatments of quasianalytic classes under suitable closure assumptions; see \cite{BelottoBierstoneChow2018}. Quasianalytic local rings and division phenomena have a substantial independent literature; see \cite{Thilliez2008}. Resolution and monomialization produce monomial forms after controlled modifications; see \cite{BierstoneMilman2004,BelottoBierstone2023}. None of those ingredients is claimed here as new.
-
-Conversely, the proofs of Theorems~\ref{thm:T2} and \ref{thm:T3} deliberately avoid coordinate division and resolution. Their input is the first nonzero Taylor polynomial at a point. The Denjoy--Carleman classification then uses only the classical quasianalyticity criterion and cutoff existence. This separation gives a self-contained proof of consequences of the earlier signature-flatness and ultradifferentiable existence results. The failure of a residual-unit factorization does not prevent the squared-multiplier reduction to~\cite{BrogliaTognoli1989}.
+Stronger division, quasianalytic local-ring, resolution, and monomialization frameworks are available~\cite{Thilliez2008,BierstoneMilman2004,BelottoBierstoneChow2018,BelottoBierstone2023}. The proofs here deliberately avoid that machinery: the rigidity input is only the first nonzero Taylor polynomial, and the realization input is only the classical Denjoy--Carleman criterion together with cutoffs or positive-flat functions. This is the sense in which the note is self-contained without claiming foundational priority.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-The finite-jet proof gives a short route to an established smooth sign obstruction and its affine-arrangement form. A nonzero leading Taylor term forces affine parity on orthants; local crossing parities propagate along each hyperplane. Its elementary nature separates this mechanism from stronger division and resolution frameworks.
-
-Combined with the classical Denjoy--Carleman criterion and positive flat functions, the argument gives an explicit realization classification. For $m$ distinct affine hyperplanes with $r$ chambers, a full normalized Roumieu class realizes $2^{m+1}$ signed-parity laws in the quasianalytic regime and all $2^r$ chamber labelings in the non-quasianalytic regime.
+A first nonzero Taylor term provides a compact bridge from local regularity to global chamber signs. In finite order it yields explicit $C^d$ orthant and $C^N$ arrangement obstructions; in the smooth setting the same mechanism gives an elementary proof of the established signature-flatness consequence for affine arrangements. Combined with the Denjoy--Carleman criterion, the argument gives an exact realization classification: for $m$ distinct hyperplanes with $r$ chambers, the full normalized Roumieu class realizes exactly $2^{m+1}$ signed parity laws in the quasianalytic case and all $2^r$ chamber labelings in the non-quasianalytic case.
 
-The contribution of the note is therefore not the first-nonzero-Taylor-term idea itself, but its systematic arrangement-level packaging, the finite-regularity orthant and arrangement forms, the explicit selector formulas, and the organized Denjoy--Carleman synthesis with exact realizability counts. The surrounding literature on distributions of signs, signatures, positive-flat functions, and chamber-function algebras makes clear that neither the underlying $C^\infty$ obstruction nor the existence mechanism for arbitrary non-quasianalytic chamber signs originates here. Real zero support need not imply original-coordinate unit factorization, but that distinction does not prevent the reduction to earlier signature-flatness results. The note is therefore positioned around transparency, finite-order formulation, and explicit synthesis rather than a claim of first discovery.
+The note's contribution is therefore the finite-order formulation, the direct affine-arrangement proof, and the explicit synthesis of rigidity, construction, and exact counting in one normalization. The underlying smooth obstruction and positive-flat existence theory are classical and are used here as historical and technical context rather than as claims of first discovery.
 
